// # EVENTS In JS :

// -> The change in the state of an obj. is known as an EVENT.
// -> EVENTS are fired to notify code of "INTRESTING CHANGES" that may affect code execution.
// Mouse events(click, double-click, etc)
// Keyboard events(keypress, keyup, keydown)
// Form events(sumit, etc)
// Print events & many more

// Until now, we've used JavaScript to change the webpage.
// Now we make the webpage respond to the user.

// For ex:
// User clicks a button → change text
// User types → update something
// User submits a form → validate it
// User moves the mouse → change styling

// These actions are called EVENTS.

// 1. What is an EVENT?
// -> An event is something that happens in the webpage.

// click       → user clicks something
// input       → user types in an input
// change      → input value changes
// submit      → form is submitted
// mouseover   → mouse moves over an element
// keydown     → keyboard key is pressed

// -> Event = something that happens


// 2. addEventListener()

// -> addEventListener() tells JavaScript: 
// -> "When this event happens, execute this function."

// SYNTAX :
// node.addEventListener(event, callback)
// callback is also known as Event Handler , it is used to execute func. inside an event
element.addEventListener("event", function() {
    // code to execute
});

// There are three important parts:
// element
//    ↓
// addEventListener
//    ↓
// event + function


// 3. Simple click example :

// <button id="btn">Click Me</button>

let button = document.querySelector("#btn"); // We select the button

button.addEventListener("click", function() { // We're telling JavaScript: "Watch this button. If someone clicks it, run this function."
    console.log("Button Clicked"); // runs only when the button is clicked.
});


// 4. Very important: The function isn't called immediately

// Look at :
button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    console.log("Button Clicked");
});

// We do not write:
// function();

// -> Instead, we're giving the function to the event listener.

// Page loads
//     ↓
// Nothing happens

// User clicks button
//     ↓
// "click" event occurs
//     ↓
// function runs
//     ↓
// "Button clicked!" printed


// 5. Practical DOM example :

// <h1 id="heading">Hello</h1>
// <button id="btn">Change Text</button>

let heading = document.querySelector("#heading");
let button = document.querySelector("#btn");

button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    heading.textContent = "Welcome to JS!";
});

// Initially: 
// Hello
// [Change Text]

// After Clicking:
// Welcome to JavaScript!
// [Change Text]

// -> So now we're combining concepts you've already learned:
// querySelector()
//       ↓
// addEventListener()
//       ↓
//     click
//       ↓
//  textContent

// -> 🔥 This is the point where DOM manipulation becomes interactive.

// 🧠 Remember this pattern :

element.addEventListener("event", function() {
    // what should happen
});

// For a button:
button.addEventListener("click", function() {
    // action
});



