// # The Event Object in JavaScript :

// -> The event object contains information about an event that occurred,
//  such as which element triggered it and what type of event happened.

// 1. What is the event object?
// -> Whenever an event occurs, JavaScript can automatically provide an object containing details about that event.

// -> We usually receive it as a parameter named event or e.
button.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    console.log(event);
});

// Here:
// -> "click" is the event we're listening for.
// -> function(event) receives information about the event.
// -> console.log(event) displays that information in the console.

// event is just a parameter name; you can also call it e.
button.addEventListener("click", function(e) {
    console.log(e);
});
// Both versions work the same way.


// 2. event.type :
// -> The type property tells us which event occurred.

// <button id="btn">Click Me</button>

let button = document.querySelector("#btn");

button.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    console.log(event.type);
});
// When you click the button, the console displays:
// Console output
// click
// -> Other event types include input, change, submit, and keydown.

 
// 3. event.target :
// -> event.target tells us which element triggered the event.

// <div id="container">
//    <button id="btn">Click Me</button>
// </div>

let container = document.querySelector("#container");

container.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    console.log(event.target);
});
// -> If you click the button, event.target refers to the button element,
//  even though the event listener is attached to the container.

// -> Illustration: clicking the button triggers the listener on its parent container,
//  and the target is the button.


// 4. event.currentTarget :
// -> This property tells us which element the event listener is attached to.

container.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    console.log(event.target);
    console.log(event.currentTarget);
});

// If you click the button inside the container:
// -> event.target → the button
// -> event.currentTarget → the container

// 1. event.type => Type of event, ex: "click"
// 2. event.target => Element that triggered the event
// 3. event.currentTarget => Element whose listener is currently running


// 5. Using the event object in a practical example :

// -> Suppose you have multiple buttons and want to identify which one was clicked.

// <button class="choice">Apple</button>
// <button class="choice">Mango</button>
// <button class="choice">Banana</button>

let buttons = document.querySelector(".choice");

buttons.forEach(function(button) {
    button.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
        console.log(event.target.textContent);
    });
});

// When you click Mango, the console displays: Mango
// -> This works because event.target identifies the clicked button, 
// and textContent reads its text.



// 6. event.preventDefault() :

// -> Another useful event method is preventDefault().
// -> It prevents the browser's default action for an event.

// For example, clicking a link normally navigates to another page.

// <a href="https://example.com" id="myLink">Visit website</a>

let link = document.querySelector("#myLink");

link.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    console.log("Navigation Prevented!");
});

// -> The link's default navigation is prevented, and the message is printed instead.
// -> This is especially useful with forms, where you may want to validate input before allowing submission.


